LLaMA-3.1-8B & 51.09{\scriptsize$\pm$2.92}$^{(3)}$ & 56.54{\scriptsize$\pm$0.64}$^{(3)}$ & $-$2.17{\scriptsize$\pm$0.76}$^{(3)}$ & +5.45 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Inference-time: same RSFT model with vs.\ without evidence output. No-evi eval uses \texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048; with-evi eval uses 1024 (1.7B uses 2048 for both). Tax $=$ no-evi $-$ with-evi; positive $=$ cost. Seeds: 1.7B s42; 4B Method B s44; 8B, 14B: s42; LLaMA: three-seed mean. Training-time tax (matched@2048 fair evaluation) in Appendix~\ref{sec:training_tax}.\par}
\end{table}


\subsection{Evidence Strategy Comparison}
\label{sec:evidence_strategy}

We compare joint generation against post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$), using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam}. Post-hoc retrieval achieves higher Rel-F1 (48.76) by avoiding the evidence tax but lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 34.7 (EDCR 0.766). RSFT CED reaches 36.1 (EDCR 0.654), a 1.4-point evidence advantage. CED reranking at inference (best-of-$N{=}8$ by CED score) achieves Rel-F1 48.64 and Evi-F1 81.88 (TP-only), approaching no-evidence extraction quality while preserving evidence.


\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}) shows the largest RSFT gain (+5.45pp) with complete pairwise seed separation (\S\ref{sec:scaling}). The RSFT improvement transfers across datasets: on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred}, RSFT achieves 41.45 Ign-F1 (+4.86pp over SFT 36.59), matching the DocRED gain (+5.08pp). Our best joint Evi-F1 (36.1, DREEAM-protocol) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.01\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). Full results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.

